From cef611e09fe5f7fd53519408def28c41802b803b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 08:50:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] player, player information and interface: the integration
 with the rest of 1.0 RC6
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

What the integration of this change resolved, as one commit after its own.

18 commits on the base of 1.0 RC6: "Dolby Vision output (this video)" applied
to the video that plays (only the video stream opened again, the sound
left playing), also for HDR10 and HDR10+, remembered for each file, with
its button in the video OSD; the Dolby Vision page of the player
information without what the overview says; the real layout of audio
that is passed through, the beds and objects of Atmos and the dialnorm;
why "Sync playback to display" decodes; the labels named as in the
CoreELEC builds; a dialog that does not finish closing no longer leaves
Kodi deaf; only the items that change are marked dirty when the focus
moves (Markus Härer, xbmc master 85a67f8b79b8); the line of a text
subtitle read before a seek given back after it; whole-number multiples
of the refresh rate keep the mode (videoscreen.hadeswholemultiple, off);
which edition of a Matroska file to play (videoplayer.hadesmkveditions,
off); and three fixes of the start signal, the wait for a buffer and
ALSA's unnamed stereo.

Conflicts:
- VideoPlayerSubtitle.cpp, the decoding of a packet: the change of the discs tags
  each overlay with its subtitle stream (cinemaONE, xbmc PR 29060),
  this side moved the decoding into DecodePacket() and remembers text
  packets for a seek. DecodePacket() is this side's, with the two lines
  that tag each overlay with its stream, so that the packets given back
  after a seek are tagged too. In OpenStream() both: the stream counted,
  then the memory cleared.
- WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp, DVBRIDGE::Hdr10AsDolbyVision():
  the change of Dolby Vision and HDR10+ made an HDR10 video go out as Dolby Vision measured where
  the output cannot send HDR10 although the setting is off, this side
  lets the choice for the video (MEASURED) stand for the setting. Both:
  any choice but AUTOMATIC and MEASURED says no; MEASURED, or AUTOMATIC
  with the setting on, as before (Hdr10AsDolbyVisionFor()); AUTOMATIC
  with the setting off, the case of the change of Dolby Vision and HDR10+ where the output cannot
  send HDR10.
- the Estuary strings of en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx: both sides
  added at the end, both kept (the other side first). This side's ids
  #32250 to #32253 and #32265 to #32266 are ids of the change of Dolby Vision and HDR10+ too: the
  renumbering is a commit of its own after the merges.
---
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
index a927093..10303e3 100644
--- a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
@@ -2509,17 +2509,18 @@ bool DVBRIDGE::Hdr10AsDolbyVision()
   const OutputChoice choice = GetOutputChoice();
   const bool setting =
       BoolSetting(SettingsNow(), CSettings::SETTING_VIDEOSCREEN_HADESDVFROMHDR10, false);
-  if (!Hdr10AsDolbyVisionFor(setting, choice, true))
+  if (choice != OutputChoice::AUTOMATIC && choice != OutputChoice::MEASURED)
     return false;
   auto* window = dynamic_cast<CWinSystemGbmGLESContext*>(CServiceBroker::GetWinSystem());
   if (!window)
     return false;
-  if (BoolSetting(SettingsNow(), CSettings::SETTING_VIDEOSCREEN_HADESDVFROMHDR10, false))
+  if (Hdr10AsDolbyVisionFor(setting, choice, true))
   {
     g_dvHdr10Needed = false;
     return window->SupportsNativeDVOutput(true);
   }
-  // Hades: where this output cannot send HDR10 at all (its connector has no HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA, as
+  // Hades: with the setting off and no choice made for the video (AUTOMATIC), where this output
+  // cannot send HDR10 at all (its connector has no HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA, as
   // i915 has none for HDMI of display version 9 in Linux 7.3, intel_hdmi.c) and the display takes
   // Dolby Vision, an HDR10 video goes out as Dolby Vision measured from its picture, as "Dolby Vision
   // as HDR10 when needed" does the other way: without it Kodi maps it to SDR. Not where the display
